T*
(assurance question. Stronger actionability generally requires stronger identity isolation,) Tj
T*
(meaningful approval, telemetry, revocation, resource bounds and recovery capability.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(4.3 Path validation) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(A material path requires evidence for every consequential relationship and condition) Tj
T*
([ATG-6]. Unsupported conditions remain UNKNOWN and constrain the path state. This) Tj
T*
(requirement is central because graph analysis can otherwise overstate exposure by treating) Tj
T*
(every theoretical route as active.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(For example, a graph may show an agent connected to a payment API. That alone does not prove) Tj
T*
(the agent can execute a transaction. Relevant conditions may include authentication, allowed) Tj
T*
(operations, transaction thresholds, human approval, environment, rate limits and runtime) Tj
T*
(policy. The assurance conclusion should reflect what is actually demonstrated.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(4.4 Alternate and residual paths) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Breaking one path does not necessarily remove exposure. A control breakpoint analysis should) Tj
T*
(consider alternate routes and the residual path that remains after intervention. If a) Tj
T*
(workflow blocks one tool but another tool reaches the same target, the system may still have) Tj
T*
(equivalent effective authority. Likewise, a human approval gate may be nominally present but) Tj
T*
(ineffective if the workflow can bypass it through another integration.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
